"""
Writes EVIDENCE.md, memo_to_ritu.md, DECISIONS.md, recording_script.md and submission-form.md
from outputs/evaluation_results.json, so that no number in the documents is typed by hand.
Run after training:  python -m src.make_docs
(The first version of the documents had hand-typed numbers; several were wrong.)
"""
import json
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
R = json.loads((ROOT / "outputs" / "evaluation_results.json").read_text())

inr = lambda x: f"Rs {x:,.0f}" if x >= 0 else f"-Rs {abs(x):,.0f}"
pct = lambda x, d=1: f"{100 * x:.{d}f}%"

dq, rg, pt, q = R["data_quality"], R["regime"], R["partners"], R["queue"]
FEB, MJ, JUN = R["eval_pre_regime_Feb_Apr"], R["eval_May_Jun"], R["eval_Jun"]
BYM = R["eval_by_month"]
LAB = R["model_labels"]

# ---- derived quantities used in several places --------------------------
rate = lambda a: a[1] / a[0]
jun_f = JUN["final"]
avg_caught = jun_f["fraud_rupees_caught"] / jun_f["frauds_in_queue"]           # Rs per fraud caught in June queue
EXP_P = 0.25                                                                   # stated expectation, see form
exp_frauds = 40 * EXP_P
exp_caught = exp_frauds * avg_caught
exp_fp = 40 - exp_frauds
exp_net_goodwill = exp_caught - exp_fp * 380
exp_net_full = exp_net_goodwill - 40 * 260
be_goodwill = 380 / (avg_caught + 380)
be_full = (380 + 260) / (avg_caught + 380)
fraud_per_month = rg["post_may_fraud_total_rs"] / rg["post_may_months"]
gross_per_review = exp_caught / 40
top5 = pt["post_may_small_top5"]
top5_txt = ", ".join(f"{t['partner_id']} ({t['frauds']} of {t['claims']})" for t in top5)
ids = sorted(dq.get("injected_ids_train_description", []))
orule = R["outlet_rule"]
tq = q["test_top_queue_partners"]


def row(name, m):
    return (f"| {name} | {m['frauds_in_queue']} of {m['reviews']} | {pct(m['precision'])} | {inr(m['fraud_rupees_caught'])} | "
            f"{inr(m['net_rs_goodwill_only'])} | {inr(m['net_rs_goodwill_and_contact'])} | {m['roc_auc']:.2f} | {m['pr_auc']:.2f} |")


def table(block):
    head = ("| Model | Frauds in queue | Precision | Fraud Rs caught | Net Rs (goodwill only) | Net Rs (goodwill + Rs 260/review) | ROC-AUC | PR-AUC |\n"
            "|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n")
    return head + "\n".join(row(LAB[k], block[k]) for k in ["rand", "rules", "lr", "cb", "final"])


def ci(b, key, fmt):
    lo, hi = b["bootstrap_final"][key]
    return f"[{fmt(lo)}, {fmt(hi)}]"


# =========================================================================
EVIDENCE = f"""# Evidence pack

Every number below is generated by `python -m src.train` then `python -m src.make_docs`
(source: `outputs/evaluation_results.json`). Nothing here is typed by hand.

## 1. Bottom line

* **Accuracy is the wrong KPI.** In May-Jun 2026 "approve everything" scores {pct(MJ['accuracy_trap_final']['accuracy_predict_nobody'], 2)} accuracy and stops Rs 0 of fraud.
  The model's own review queue scores {pct(MJ['accuracy_trap_final']['accuracy_top40_flagged'], 2)} (it flags 40 claims a month as fraud, most of which are genuine). The 97% target cannot tell a useful model from a useless one: a realistic 40-a-month list scores below approve-everything.
* **The model works only when it has seen the new regime.** Trained through 31 May and tested on June it puts **{jun_f['frauds_in_queue']} of 40** reviews on real fraud
  (ROC-AUC {jun_f['roc_auc']:.2f}). Trained before 1 May and tested on May it put **0 of 40** on fraud (ROC-AUC {BYM['May']['roc_auc']:.2f}): it had never seen the post-1-May pattern. **Retrain monthly.**
* **The queue is roughly break-even, not a profit centre.** At the policy costs a review has to be fraud about {pct(be_goodwill, 0)} of the time to pay back the Rs 380 goodwill,
  and about {pct(be_full, 0)} if the Rs 260 contact cost is charged to every review. June achieved {pct(jun_f['precision'], 1)}; May 0%.
* **The strongest signal is the outlet.** {top5_txt} (post-May small claims) hold {pct(pt['post_may_small_top5_share_of_fraud'], 0)} of that fraud.
* **Evidence is thin.** 141 frauds in total, {JUN['final']['positives']} in the June fold. Intervals below are wide and we do not hide that.

## 2. Data quality (what we found, what we did)

| Item | Finding | Action |
|---|---|---|
| Duplicate claim_ids | {dq['duplicate_rows_removed']} resubmitted rows ({dq['train_rows_raw']:,} raw to {dq['train_rows_unique_claims']:,} unique) | keep last submission; a claim never sits in two folds |
| Undecided labels | {dq['undecided_blank_labels']} blank (all CRM rows) | dropped from training and evaluation, still scored if they appear |
| Legacy Zoho "0" labels | {dq['legacy_rows_decided']:,} legacy rows are all 0/1, so undecided cases there are indistinguishable from genuine 0s | kept; cannot be fixed from the data. If legacy had the same blank rate as the CRM (202 of 7,154 = 2.8%), roughly 130 of the legacy 0s would be undecided. This is an assumption, not a measurement |
| Injected instructions in free text | **{dq['injected_or_unrecognised_train_description_rows']} rows** in train ({len(ids)} distinct claims: {', '.join(ids)}; one was resubmitted), **{dq['injected_or_unrecognised_test_description_rows']} in test**. They tell an "AI reviewer" to use a random split, report accuracy only and trust partner onboarding date | free text is never a model feature or an LLM input; text is whitelist-checked |
| Serial typing mess | lower case, spaces, dashes | normalised to upper-case alphanumerics; only a validity flag (11 characters) is used |
| Claims before partner onboarding | train {dq['claims_before_partner_onboarding_train']}, test {dq['claims_before_partner_onboarding_test']} | none to fix |
| Test partners never seen in train | {dq['test_partners_not_in_train']} outlets, {dq['test_rows_unseen_partner']} claims | scored on claim facts only; flagged in API warnings |
| Timestamps | submitted_at is IST per the README. The UTC issue in the policy concerns Zoho *resolution events*, which are not in these files | nothing to convert; we say so rather than pretend |

## 3. The 1 May 2026 change

| | Claims | Frauds | Rate |
|---|---|---|---|
| Before 1 May, under Rs 2,000 | {rg['pre_small'][0]:,} | {rg['pre_small'][1]} | {pct(rate(rg['pre_small']), 2)} |
| Before 1 May, Rs 2,000 and over | {rg['pre_large'][0]:,} | {rg['pre_large'][1]} | {pct(rate(rg['pre_large']), 2)} |
| After 1 May, under Rs 2,000 | {rg['post_small'][0]:,} | {rg['post_small'][1]} | {pct(rate(rg['post_small']), 2)} |
| After 1 May, Rs 2,000 and over | {rg['post_large'][0]:,} | {rg['post_large'][1]} | {pct(rate(rg['post_large']), 2)} |
| Rs 1,900 to 1,999 (all dates) | {rg['near_2000'][0]} | {rg['near_2000'][1]} | {pct(rate(rg['near_2000']), 2)} |

Inspection of small claims fell from {pct(rg['small_pre_inspected_share'], 0)} to {pct(rg['small_post_inspected_share'], 1)}. Post-May fraud paid on decided claims: {inr(rg['post_may_fraud_total_rs'])} in two months
(about {inr(fraud_per_month)} a month; average fraud claim {inr(rg['post_may_fraud_amount_mean'])}, median {inr(rg['post_may_fraud_amount_median'])}).

## 4. How it was validated

* **Rolling-origin by month.** For each month Feb-Jun 2026: train on everything before the month, score that month. No random splits anywhere.
* **Partner history is "as-of".** Training rows see only labels from claims at least 30 days older. Validation months see a **frozen snapshot at the month start**, exactly how the live service and the test file are scored.
* **Queue = top 40 per month**, the investigation desk's limit. Ties are broken randomly.
* **Intervals** are partner-clustered bootstrap (1,000 resamples of outlets), because claims from one outlet are not independent.
* **Selection caveat.** Hyper-parameters (depth 3, 300 trees, strong L2) and the 0.7/0.3 blend were set by hand after exploratory runs on these same folds. Treat the numbers as mildly optimistic. No early stopping on any evaluation fold.
* **Features** (22 numeric + 2 categorical): amount and its distance below Rs 2,000, days since purchase vs warranty, customer prior claims, photo, partner tenure and type, product family,
  and the outlet's as-of fraud history (all claims and small claims). Dropped: inspection fields (exist only for inspected claims), free text, serial repeat counts, hour/day, volume counts.

## 5. Results

### 5.1 June 2026: the regime-aware fold (model trained through 31 May; {JUN['final']['positives']} frauds in {JUN['final']['claims']} claims; 40 reviews)
{table(JUN)}

Partner-clustered 95% intervals for the final model: precision {ci(JUN, 'precision_ci', lambda v: pct(v, 0))}, fraud rupees caught {ci(JUN, 'rupees_caught_ci', inr)}, ROC-AUC {ci(JUN, 'roc_auc_ci', lambda v: f'{v:.2f}')}.
Lift over *expected* random ({pct(JUN['random_expected_precision'])}): {JUN['lift_vs_expected_random']:.1f}x. (The random row above is one random draw, not the expectation.)

### 5.2 May + June 2026 (80 reviews, {MJ['final']['positives']} frauds)
{table(MJ)}

Intervals for the final model: precision {ci(MJ, 'precision_ci', lambda v: pct(v, 0))}, ROC-AUC {ci(MJ, 'roc_auc_ci', lambda v: f'{v:.2f}')}.

### 5.3 February to April 2026 (before the regime change; 120 reviews, {FEB['final']['positives']} frauds)
{table(FEB)}

### 5.4 Month by month (final model, 40 reviews each)
| Month | Frauds that month | Frauds in queue | ROC-AUC | Net Rs (goodwill + contact) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
""" + "\n".join(f"| {m} | {v['positives']} | {v['frauds_in_queue']} | {v['roc_auc']:.2f} | {inr(v['net_rs_goodwill_and_contact'])} |" for m, v in BYM.items()) + f"""

Months with 5-9 frauds give very noisy AUCs (February's 1.00 is five frauds); look at the totals above rather than single months.

**May is a failure and we keep it in.** The model trained before 1 May had no post-regime examples; its AUC was below 0.5 and its queue held no fraud. This is why the memo says: retrain every month.

**Ranking by expected rupees (P x amount - (1-P) x 380) did not help**: {MJ['ev_ranked']['frauds_in_queue']} frauds / {inr(MJ['ev_ranked']['net_rs_goodwill_and_contact'])} in May-Jun vs {MJ['final']['frauds_in_queue']} / {inr(MJ['final']['net_rs_goodwill_and_contact'])} for ranking by probability. We kept probability ranking.

## 6. The accuracy trap
| Strategy (May-Jun, {MJ['final']['claims']:,} claims) | Accuracy | Fraud stopped |
|---|---|---|
| Approve everything | {pct(MJ['accuracy_trap_final']['accuracy_predict_nobody'], 2)} | Rs 0 |
| Model queue (top 40 a month called "fraud") | {pct(MJ['accuracy_trap_final']['accuracy_top40_flagged'], 2)} | {inr(MJ['final']['fraud_rupees_caught'])} |

## 7. The money
* Stopped per claim reviewed, June: {inr(jun_f['fraud_rupees_caught'] / 40)} gross; {inr(jun_f['net_rs_goodwill_only'] / 40)} after goodwill; {inr(jun_f['net_rs_goodwill_and_contact'] / 40)} after goodwill and Rs 260 contact cost.
* The Rs 260 "blended cost of a service contact" is ambiguous. We show both readings instead of choosing silently.
* Break-even precision uses the June average caught fraud ({inr(avg_caught)}): {pct(be_goodwill, 0)} (goodwill only), {pct(be_full, 0)} (goodwill + contact).
* Expectation for a test month at {pct(EXP_P, 0)} precision: {exp_frauds:.0f} frauds caught = {inr(exp_caught)} stopped, {exp_fp:.0f} false holds = {inr(exp_fp * 380)} goodwill. Net {inr(exp_net_goodwill)} (goodwill only) or {inr(exp_net_full)} (with Rs 260 per review).
* February-April (before the change, larger claims): net {inr(FEB['final']['net_rs_goodwill_and_contact'])} over 120 reviews.

## 8. Is it the newer partners?
* New outlets (onboarded since 1 Jul 2025): {pt['new_with_any_fraud']} of {pt['new_with_labelled_claims']} with labelled claims have any fraud; {pt['new_partners_listed'] - pt['new_with_labelled_claims']} of the {pt['new_partners_listed']} listed have no labelled claims at all ({pt['new_partners_onboarded_after_train_end']} were onboarded after the data ends). "Clean" for those means "no data", not "verified clean".
* Fraud rate on claims: new outlets {pct(pt['fraud_rate_new'])} vs older outlets {pct(pt['fraud_rate_old'])}. So Ritu's instinct has some support in the rate, and Meenal is right that most new outlets are fine: {pt['new_with_labelled_claims'] - pt['new_with_any_fraud']} of {pt['new_with_labelled_claims']} new outlets with claims have no fraud.
* The five outlets behind most post-May small-claim fraud were all onboarded Nov 2025 - Feb 2026: {', '.join(f"{k} ({v})" for k, v in pt['top5_onboarded'].items())}. Concentration: the top 12 outlets hold {pct(pt['top12_partner_fraud_share'], 0)} of all fraud.
* Onboarding date alone is not a usable rule: it would flag {pt['new_partners_listed']} outlets to reach a handful.

## 9. Smaller checks (read with care: June has {JUN['final']['positives']} frauds, a change of 1-3 frauds is noise)
* **Ablation, June fold:** """ + "; ".join(f"{k}: {v['frauds_in_top40']} of 40 (AUC {v['roc_auc']:.2f})" for k, v in R['ablation_Jun'].items()) + f""".
* **Stale snapshot:** the test file is scored with a snapshot that ages up to 3 months. June fold with a snapshot 0 / 30 / 60 days old: """ + " / ".join(f"{v['frauds_in_top40']} of 40" for v in R['staleness_Jun'].values()) + f""" in the queue. No visible damage in this one fold.
* **Unseen outlets (May-Jun):** {R['unseen_partners_May_Jun']['claims']} claims from {R['unseen_partners_May_Jun']['partners']} outlets with no earlier labelled claims, {R['unseen_partners_May_Jun']['frauds']} frauds. Too few to say anything about cold-start performance.
* **Simple outlet rule (no model):** re-inspect small claims from outlets with 3 or more confirmed frauds at the start of the month. May: {orule['May']['claims_to_inspect']} claims, {orule['May']['frauds_inside']} frauds (the wave had not shown up yet).
  June: {orule['Jun']['claims_to_inspect']} claims from {orule['Jun']['outlets']} outlets, {orule['Jun']['frauds_inside']} frauds ({pct(orule['Jun']['precision'], 0)}), {pct(orule['Jun']['share_of_month_frauds'], 0)} of June's frauds, {inr(orule['Jun']['fraud_rupees_inside'])}.

## 10. What the test predictions look like
* {R['predictions']['rows']:,} claims, {R['predictions']['distinct_scores']:,} distinct scores in [{R['predictions']['min']:.4f}, {R['predictions']['max']:.4f}], mean {R['predictions']['mean']:.3f} (close to the expected fraud rate; scores are probabilities, not rank-compressed).
* Queue threshold {q['threshold']:.3f} flags {', '.join(f'{k}: {v}' for k, v in q['test_flags_per_month'].items())} claims per month (target 40).
* Queue concentration: """ + ", ".join(f"{k} {v}" for k, v in tq.items()) + """ claims. Two of these (SP3118, SP3286) were onboarded in June 2026 and have only two labelled claims, both fraud: strong but thin evidence. SP3300 was onboarded 29 Jul 2026 and has no history, so it is flagged on claim facts alone. Review these by hand before acting on them.

## 11. Known weaknesses
* 141 frauds; June fold has 22. All intervals are wide.
* One regime-consistent fold only (June). May shows the model fails if it is stale.
* Hand-set blend and hyper-parameters (see section 4).
* The test period (Jul-Sep) is scored with a snapshot frozen on 30 Jun, and its true fraud labels will arrive 30+ days late.
* Fraud rate in the test months is unknown; the stated expectation assumes it stays near the post-May level.
"""

